1
00:00:06,200 --> 00:00:10,600
Training a large model costs real money, so we want to train fast.

2
00:00:10,600 --> 00:00:15,433
Today the default is AdamW with carefully tuned hyperparameters.

3
00:00:15,433 --> 00:00:20,500
A new optimizer needs its own hyperparameter search, and that search is difficult.

4
00:00:20,500 --> 00:00:28,933
Worse, under Adam we often see lazy training: the model barely moves from its random initialization.

5
00:00:28,933 --> 00:00:36,133
Initialization rules were a relief. Before them everything was standard normal. Can updates be as principled?

6
00:00:36,133 --> 00:00:41,133
Write theta for all the parameters and L for the average loss over the data.

7
00:00:41,133 --> 00:00:47,033
Near the current point, the loss looks like a line: its value plus the gradient times the step.

8
00:00:47,033 --> 00:00:49,933
For a small step, the line is accurate.

9
00:00:49,933 --> 00:00:55,833
For a big step, the line lies to us: it promises a much lower loss than we get.

10
00:00:55,833 --> 00:01:03,033
So a step must be small enough for the approximation to hold, yet large enough to converge fast.

11
00:01:03,033 --> 00:01:08,233
For least squares on one sample, the gradient is the residual times the input.

12
00:01:08,233 --> 00:01:12,266
SGD steps against it, scaled by the learning rate eta.

13
00:01:12,266 --> 00:01:19,766
With these numbers the residual is minus three, and one step moves theta to zero point three, zero point six.

14
00:01:19,766 --> 00:01:26,166
But why this direction and this length? Let's derive a step instead of guessing one.

15
00:01:26,166 --> 00:01:32,733
Ask directly: among all steps of size at most eta, which lowers the linearized loss the most?

16
00:01:32,733 --> 00:01:38,433
The loss value itself is a constant, so only the inner product with the gradient matters.

17
00:01:38,433 --> 00:01:48,233
Take two parameters, gradient g = (2, −0.5). Choice one: change each by at most eta: a square.

18
00:01:48,233 --> 00:01:54,733
Slide a line of constant inner product against the gradient, and stop when it is about to leave the square.

19
00:01:54,733 --> 00:02:01,233
It leaves through a corner: every coordinate moves by eta, against the sign of its gradient.

20
00:02:01,233 --> 00:02:06,933
That step is minus eta times the sign of the gradient. It is called sign SGD.

21
00:02:06,933 --> 00:02:13,466
Predicted change: minus two plus minus a half, that is minus 2.5 eta.

22
00:02:13,466 --> 00:02:19,100
Choice two: bound the ordinary length instead. Now the allowed set is a circle.

23
00:02:19,100 --> 00:02:24,333
The line now last touches the circle where the step points straight against the gradient.

24
00:02:24,333 --> 00:02:33,433
Cauchy-Schwarz: an inner product is largest in size when the vectors are aligned. So the step is minus eta times the unit-length gradient.

25
00:02:33,433 --> 00:02:43,066
That is gradient descent with a normalized step. Predicted change: minus 2.06 eta, a bit less than the sign step.

26
00:02:43,066 --> 00:02:49,566
A cousin of this idea: instead of a hard bound, penalize the step with lambda times its squared length.

27
00:02:49,566 --> 00:02:52,266
Set its gradient to zero and solve.

28
00:02:52,266 --> 00:02:58,700
The minimizer is minus one over two lambda times the gradient. That is plain gradient descent.

29
00:02:58,700 --> 00:03:06,466
Its learning rate is one over two lambda, so sweeping lambda sweeps out the same solutions as eta.

30
00:03:06,466 --> 00:03:13,733
Notice the sign step is longer: its corner sits at root two, about 1.41, times eta.

31
00:03:13,733 --> 00:03:22,133
With d parameters it is root d times eta. Same eta, different norm, different meaning of small.

32
00:03:22,133 --> 00:03:30,600
This is the recipe of Bernstein and Newhouse, 2024: choose a norm, choose a step size, and an optimizer falls out.

33
00:03:30,600 --> 00:03:37,800
The infinity norm gives sign SGD, the two norm gives gradient descent, and the spectral norm is coming up.

34
00:03:37,800 --> 00:03:44,500
The right norm may depend on the geometry and architecture of the network. That is where we go next.

